PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"At 320 PM MDT, Gusty winds in and near the windier downslope areas of\nthe Front Range Mountains and Foothills will continue into late this\nafternoon and possibly early evening. This includes locations along\nthe Peak to Peak Highway, Highway 93 between Boulder and Golden, and\nU.S. 36 from Broomfield to Estes Park.\n\nHAZARD...Wind gusts of 50 to 55 mph.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Gusty winds could knock down small tree limbs and blow\naround unsecured objects. Hazardous travel conditions can be\nexpected for high profile vehicles.\n\nLocations impacted include...\nLakewood, Arvada, Westminster, southern Boulder, southwestern\nBroomfield, Wheat Ridge, Golden, Louisville, Superior, Trail Ridge,\nIdaho Springs, Nederland, Bear Lake, Winter Park, Central City,\nEmpire, Jamestown, Ward and Black Hawk.","ends":"2026-08-30T17:15:00-06:00","severity":"Moderate","areaDesc":"Jackson County Below 9000 Feet; South and East Jackson/Larimer/North and Northeast Grand/Northwest Boulder Counties Above 9000 Feet; South and Southeast Grand/West Central and Southwest Boulder/Gilpin/Clear Creek/Summit/North and West Park Counties Above 9000 Feet; Larimer and Boulder Counties Between 6000 and 9000 Feet; Jefferson and West Douglas Counties Above  6000 Feet/Gilpin/Clear Creek/Northeast Park Counties Below 9000 Feet; Boulder And Jefferson Counties Below 6000 Feet/West Broomfield County","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0a7c318642c923f444fdec18aa60b06dc1621661.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.0a7c318642c923f444fdec18aa60b06dc1621661.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued August 30 at 11:36AM MDT until September 1 at 12:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas,\nCentral Colorado River Basin, Central Gunnison and Uncompahgre\nRiver Basin, Central Yampa River Basin, Debeque to Silt Corridor,\nFlat Tops, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Gore and Elk\nMountains/Central Mountain Valleys, Grand Valley, Grand and\nBattlement Mesas, Northwest San Juan Mountains, Paradox\nValley/Lower Dolores River, Roan and Tavaputs Plateaus, Southwest\nSan Juan Mountains, Uncompahgre Plateau and Dallas Divide, Upper\nGunnison River Valley and West Elk and Sawatch Mountains and Utah,\nincluding the following areas, Arches/Grand Flat,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains, Southeast\nUtah and Tavaputs Plateau.\n\n* WHEN...Through Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\nRecent burn scars will also be especially susceptible and these\ninclude the Lee and Elk RX, Gold Mountain and Elk, Ferris, and\nBabylon burn scars to name a few.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Monsoonal moisture will remain across the area through Monday\nevening and will support thunderstorms and showers with heavy\nrainfall across the watch area. Several rounds of heavier rain\nare expected increasing the chance for flash flooding especially\nin our more susceptible areas.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-01T00:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Central Yampa River Basin; Roan and Tavaputs Plateaus; Grand Valley; Debeque to Silt Corridor; Central Colorado River Basin; Grand and Battlement Mesas; Gore and Elk Mountains/Central Mountain Valleys; Central Gunnison and Uncompahgre River Basin; West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Flat Tops; Upper Gunnison River Valley; Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Southeast Utah; Tavaputs Plateau; Arches/Grand Flat; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bf53ca765d0cc18c1b080bdda7ac3b081a211a37.002.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.bf53ca765d0cc18c1b080bdda7ac3b081a211a37.002.1"}],"afd":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-08-30T19:48:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KBOU 301948 AFDBOU Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Denver/Boulder CO 148 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Scattered showers and storms capable of producing strong to severe wind gusts are expected to continue through the evening. - Better precipitation chances on Monday, with another round of afternoon/evening showers and storms, a few may become severe. - War","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"BOU","issued":"2026-08-30T20:02:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION...Rest of today through Monday... Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected to continue across the mountains today, with scattered showers and storms across the lower elevations. There are good chances (40-60%) of wetting rainfall across most of the high country north of I-70. Minimum humidity should remain above 20% across the plains and generally will remain near or above 40%","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=BOU&issuedby=BOU&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"gby01","station_name":"Granby","distance_km":13.6,"soil_temp_5cm_f":65.12,"soil_temp_15cm_f":60.8,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":552,"wind_speed_mph":3.11,"air_temp_f":59.2,"relative_humidity":0.696,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu/station/gby01"},"aviation":{"icao":"KAEJ","distance_km":0,"raw_metar":"METAR KAEJ 302235Z AUTO 33022G27KT 10SM SCT090 21/04 A3030 RMK AO2 T02050044","temp_f":69,"wind_dir":330,"wind_kt":22,"gust_kt":27,"visibility":"10+","cover":"SCT","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1026.2,"observed":1788129300,"raw_taf":null,"url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KAEJ&hours=0"}